WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.729
Many Axum services connect web requests to
trusted systems: databases,

2
00:00:07.809 --> 00:00:15.985
queues, caches, file systems, object
stores, search indexes, and internal APIs.

3
00:00:16.065 --> 00:00:24.242
The handler is often the bridge between
untrusted request data and sensitive data

4
00:00:24.322 --> 00:00:25.625
operations.

5
00:00:25.705 --> 00:00:32.815
That bridge should be narrow, validated,
authorized, and observable.

6
00:00:32.895 --> 00:00:37.753
Resource identifiers need defensive
handling.

7
00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:43.166
A parsed identifier is not automatically
safe to use.

8
00:00:43.246 --> 00:00:50.474
The application should validate that the
identifier has the right form,

9
00:00:50.554 --> 00:00:57.427
belongs to the correct tenant or owner, is
allowed for the operation,

10
00:00:57.507 --> 00:01:03.077
and is checked before sensitive retrieval
or mutation.

11
00:01:03.157 --> 00:01:11.096
Authorization should not be an
afterthought placed after data has already been

12
00:01:11.176 --> 00:01:12.954
fetched broadly.

13
00:01:13.034 --> 00:01:19.670
Parameterized data access patterns are
part of defensive design.

14
00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:25.912
The course does not need offensive
examples to make the point:

15
00:01:25.992 --> 00:01:30.850
string-built queries, fragile resource
logic,

16
00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:38.633
and inconsistent filtering are harder to
review than structured query APIs,

17
00:01:38.713 --> 00:01:42.860
parameter binding, typed repositories,

18
00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:48.628
and domain methods that require
authorization context.

19
00:01:48.708 --> 00:01:57.240
Reviewers should be able to see how
user-controlled values reach data access layers.

20
00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:01.586
Async services also need resource safety.

21
00:02:01.666 --> 00:02:09.487
Memory safety does not prevent an
application from accepting unlimited work,

22
00:02:09.568 --> 00:02:17.033
waiting forever on upstream dependencies,
exhausting a connection pool,

23
00:02:17.113 --> 00:02:22.564
or spawning background tasks with unclear
ownership.

24
00:02:22.644 --> 00:02:29.162
Connection pools, queue sizes, request
bodies, task lifetimes,

25
00:02:29.242 --> 00:02:36.944
and upstream calls all need limits and
timeout behavior aligned with service

26
00:02:37.024 --> 00:02:38.328
objectives.

27
00:02:38.408 --> 00:02:47.295
Backpressure and bounded concurrency are
security-relevant because availability is a

28
00:02:47.375 --> 00:02:49.390
security property.

29
00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:56.698
A service should have a plan for what
happens when dependencies are slow,

30
00:02:56.778 --> 00:03:02.703
request volume rises, queues fill, or
clients disconnect.

31
00:03:02.783 --> 00:03:09.064
Defensive Axum design treats async work as
a managed resource,

32
00:03:09.144 --> 00:03:12.580
not an infinite capacity promise.
